# Wave Drain

Drain chooses work; Ship delivers it. Do not create a second lifecycle, reviewer policy,
evidence store, worktree manager, or landing path here.

## 1. Select ready work

List open issues and exclude `blocked`, `needs-human`, `needs-clarification`, and `epic`.
Before selection, require explicit `AC-N` acceptance criteria, non-goals, and affected
files/contracts. Run `scripts/issue-readiness.mjs` when present. Missing evidence or an unavailable
issue source is unknown, so exclude the issue from this wave and record why.

### Phase 0.5 — Harvest the finding spool

Before selection, inspect incidental findings left by earlier work with `arbiter finding list`,
then use `arbiter finding promote` to revalidate, deduplicate, and file ready findings through the
normal issue workflow. A missing or empty spool is a clean no-op.

Worker DONE reports use the `FindingEntry` shape from `src/commands/task-note.ts`; do not invent a
second finding schema. `graphNode` and `auditorHint` are optional; every other field is required:

```json
{
  "ts": "2026-06-16T00:00:00.000Z",
  "note": "duplicated retry helper",
  "kind": "dup",
  "severity": "low",
  "foundDuring": "#1404",
  "file": "src/example.ts",
  "line": 120,
  "sha": "deadbeefcafef00d",
  "graphNode": "src/example.ts#retry",
  "fingerprint": "<sha1 of kind+normalizedPath+symbol+normalizedNote>",
  "auditorHint": "code-quality"
}
```

## 2. Compose a capability train

Select at most the configured `ship.train.maxChain` or `--wave-size`, whichever is smaller.
Every joined issue must satisfy the Ship affinity proof:

- same user-visible outcome;
- related owner paths or dependencies;
- compatible ordering;
- shared proof, acceptance boundary, and rollback boundary;
- no hard conflict.

Uncertain affinity seals the train. Conflicting work lands separately. Each joined issue keeps
namespaced acceptance criteria, RED evidence, a commit reference, and a closing reference.
An issue declaring `conflicts-with:#N` shares a serial lane with `#N`; it never enters a parallel
lane.

Record one cumulative plan with the complete file manifest, dependency proof, acceptance criteria,
non-goals, rollback, and issue-to-proof mapping. Mechanical admission checks validate the plan and
finish before implementation.

## 3. Invoke the shared Ship contract

Start the train through the same runtime entrypoint as a single issue:

```bash
arbiter ship #A #B --tier <XS|S|Standard> --affinity '<json>'
```

Follow the emitted next action and advance only through:

```bash
arbiter ship --advance
```

Ship owns persisted treatment, TDD transitions, review policy, exact-subject evidence, recovery,
PR, CI, merge, post-merge proof, issue closure, and cleanup. Never recompute its tier or reviewer
panel from this skill.

## 4. Optional parallel implementation

Under the ADR-103 carve-out, parallel writers are allowed only when every lane has a dedicated
worktree, a distinct branch, and plan-manifest-disjoint file-sets. Dependency files, main-tree
changes, tags, and overlapping paths remain serial. This is the owner-ratified 2026-07-10
convergence model: governed repositories produce ONE wave PR. One author owns each worktree. Each
lane runs TDD and targeted checks; the integrated frozen candidate receives the single final
qualification owned by Ship.

`--max-parallel` defaults to **<%= maxParallelWorktrees %>**. The effective cap is
`min(--max-parallel, nproc - 2, wave size)` so workers retain machine headroom.

Expensive same-repository gates run through `arbiter check run -- <cmd>` and its `flock(1)` mutex.
The lock releases when the gate-exec supervisor is SIGKILL/OOM-killed; killing the Arbiter Node PID
alone leaves that supervisor holding. Without `flock(1)`, fail closed to `--max-parallel 1`.
Worktrees use `symlink-children` plus `VITE_CACHE_DIR="$PWD/.cache/vite"` so their caches do not race.

Gate-wait is one deterministic foreground wait. Turn-stall is different: bound it with the
watchdog sweep over real PR, issue, worktree, and DONE-report state; never poll the mutex.

Route every wave worktree through the native host and the canonical
`arbiter worktree prepare/check/relink` helpers; do not hand-roll worktree commands.

**M16 handoff-contract: subagents never own waits** — a worker returns its commit, tests, residual
risks, and structured evidence. The orchestrator owns integration and external waits.

Integrate lanes serially in dependency order, breaking ties by overlap computed from the REAL branch
diffs with `git diff --name-only main...<branch>`, not the plan estimate. A conflict, missing commit,
failed targeted test, or scope breach is a failure, not permission to skip proof. Close each
worktree with the native host, then run `arbiter worktree check` to verify cleanup.

## 5. Qualify and land

On the frozen integrated SHA, Ship runs one independent final review with the persisted treatment's
pertinent verticals. That same panel supplies an acceptance decision for every AC. Applicable
MED/HIGH/CRITICAL findings block; reconcile one fix batch and invalidate stale review, acceptance,
and gate evidence when source changes.

After review and all-PASS acceptance fit, run the full gate once for the exact candidate. Reuse the
unchanged receipt through PR and CI. Completion requires the PR merged, green post-merge CI, live
proof when applicable, every carried issue closed, and worktree cleanup.

Commit the cumulative candidate and all review/evidence artifacts before the one exact-HEAD gate:

```bash
arbiter check run -- node scripts/check-all.mjs L2
```

One PR per wave. For `trunk-solo + pr-ff`, use the exact-SHA watcher:

